                  x="724"
                  y="390"
                  fill="#cc0000"
                  font-size="9"
                  text-anchor="end">{shipStamp("terraform")}</text
                >
                <text x="416" y="418" fill="#6a6a6a" font-size="9"
                  >Terraform &#183; hcloud + azurerm providers</text
                >

                {/* legend */}
                <text x="20" y="464" fill="#8a8a8a" font-size="9"
                  >&#9473; RUNNING</text
                >
                <text x="110" y="464" fill="#6a6a6a" font-size="9"
                  >&#9476; SCHEDULED</text
                >
              </svg>
            </div>
            <p class="case-caption">
              The edge node accepts no inbound connections at all &mdash;
              everything it serves leaves through an outbound WireGuard session
              it opens itself. The cloud node is the one with a public face: its
              firewall allows 22, 80 and 443, and today the workload is reached
              over a NodePort bound across the tailnet. The dashed elements are
              not running yet; they land this week, on the dates stamped in their
              corners.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">The Goal</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              I wanted a cluster that behaves like a real one &mdash; two machines
              on different networks, one of them behind CGNAT with no way to
              accept inbound traffic, sharing a control plane and a single
              public entry point. Anything that only works on one laptop teaches
              you nothing about scheduling, networking or failure.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">What I Built &mdash; March 2026</h3>
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Cluster:</span> a K3s server on a Hetzner
                VPS and a K3s agent on a local Ubuntu machine, joined over a
                Tailscale WireGuard mesh with Flannel routed over the VPN. The
                node-to-node traffic is encrypted and the edge side never opens a
                port.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Service reachability:</span>
                <code>kube-proxy</code> binds the NodePort across the tailnet interface,
                so the service answers on either node's mesh address regardless of
                where the pods happen to be scheduled.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Workload:</span> deployed with
                <code>kubectl</code> against plain manifests, and autoscaled with
                <code>kubectl autoscale</code> &mdash; no Helm, no ingress
                controller, nothing between the request and the pod that I had not
                put there myself.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Load:</span> driven with ApacheBench and read
                off <code>kubectl top</code> during the run.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">Autoscaling Under Load</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              A HorizontalPodAutoscaler drives the deployment off live CPU
              metrics. I loaded it with ApacheBench across the Tailscale tunnel
              and read the reaction off the cluster rather than trusting the
              manifest.
            </p>
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Load:</span> 100 concurrent clients, 100,000
                total requests via ApacheBench.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Throughput:</span> 1718.34 req/s sustained,
                58.196 ms mean response time.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Reliability:</span> 0 failed requests out of
                100,000, every one of them over the Tailscale tunnel.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Reaction:</span> the HPA is configured
                <code>min 4 / max 20</code>; under this run it scaled 4 &rarr; 10
                replicas, spreading pods across both nodes. I did not time how
                long the scale-up took &mdash; that measurement goes in on the next
                run.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Recovery:</span> scaled back down after the
                stabilisation window once the load stopped.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">Chaos Engineering &mdash; The Useful Failure</h3>
            <div class="border border-gray-800 p-5">
              <h4 class="font-serif text-lg mb-2">
                The edge node suspended itself mid-test
              </h4>
              <p class="case-text !mb-3">
                I had one genuinely unplanned failure: the physical edge node went
                to sleep while under load. The cluster did exactly the right thing
                &mdash;
                the node went <code>NotReady</code>, its pods were evicted, and
                the deployment rescheduled them onto the cloud node without a
                request being dropped.
              </p>
              <p class="case-text !mb-3">
                <span class="case-k">The part worth understanding:</span> when
                the edge node woke up and rejoined, the pods did
                <em>not</em> come back to it. The deployment's replica count was
                already satisfied, and Kubernetes has no trigger that
                re-evaluates placement for pods that are already running happily.
                Rebalancing is not a scheduler feature &mdash; it needs something
                external such as a descheduler, or pod topology spread
                constraints applied at admission time.
              </p>
              <p class="case-text !mb-0 text-gray-400">
                That distinction &mdash; between "the scheduler failed" and "the
                scheduler was never asked again" &mdash; is the single most useful
                thing this cluster has taught me.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">Scheduled &mdash; This Week</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              None of the following is running yet. Each one goes on top of the
              cluster described above, on the date beside it, and the measured
              result replaces the plan here as it lands.
            </p>
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Helm &mdash; {shipLabel("ingress")}:</span> I
                will move the workloads off raw manifests into Helm releases with
                values pinned in the repository, so the difference between two
                deployments is a values file rather than a diff.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">TLS ingress &mdash; {shipLabel("ingress")}:</span>
                ingress-nginx becomes the single entry point, with cert-manager
                issuing and renewing Let's Encrypt certificates automatically.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k"
                  >Observability &mdash; {shipLabel("observability")}:</span
                > kube-prometheus-stack will replace the hand-read
                <code>kubectl top</code> I used in March, so the next load test is
                a dashboard rather than a sentence.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Terraform &mdash; {shipLabel("terraform")}:</span>
                the VMs, networks and firewall rules move into HCL against the
                <code>hcloud</code> provider, so rebuilding the whole thing from
                nothing is an <code>apply</code> rather than an afternoon.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Azure redeployment &mdash; {shipLabel("azure")}:</span>
                I will stand the same releases up on Azure VMs with the
                <code>azurerm</code> provider, to prove nothing quietly grew roots
                in one provider's assumptions.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

