    >
      <div data-modal-close class="absolute inset-0 bg-black/80 backdrop-blur-sm">
      </div>

      <div class="case-modal-sheet">
        <div class="case-modal-head">
          <div>
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 mb-2">
              <div class="mono-label-red !mb-0">[ Build_Plan ]</div>
              <span class="case-progress-badge case-progress-badge-planned"
                >Not Started</span
              >
            </div>
            <h2
              id="keycloak-modal-title"
              class="font-serif text-2xl md:text-3xl leading-tight"
            >
              Putting a Real Identity Provider in Front of My Own Applications
              &mdash; Building {shipLabel("keycloak")}
            </h2>
          </div>
          <button
            type="button"
            data-modal-close
            aria-label="Close case study"
            class="case-modal-close">✕</button
          >
        </div>

        <div class="px-6 py-8 md:px-10 md:py-10 space-y-10">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-3 text-center">
            <div class="border border-dashed border-gray-800 p-3">
              <div class="font-mono text-lg md:text-xl text-nothing-red">OIDC</div>
              <div class="stat-cap">Auth Code + PKCE</div>
            </div>
            <div class="border border-dashed border-gray-800 p-3">
              <div class="font-mono text-lg md:text-xl text-nothing-red">
                WebAuthn
              </div>
              <div class="stat-cap">Passwordless Login</div>
            </div>
            <div class="border border-dashed border-gray-800 p-3">
              <div class="font-mono text-lg md:text-xl text-nothing-red">SAML</div>
              <div class="stat-cap">2.0 Client, Side by Side</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">Why</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              Every project I have written stores its own users and its own
              password hashes. That is the wrong answer in almost every
              production environment, and it means I have never implemented the
              protocols enterprises actually run on. So I am going to stop writing
              authentication and start delegating it: one Keycloak realm on my
              cluster, and my applications reduced to consumers of tokens they did
              not issue.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">The Plan</h3>
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Helm:</span> Keycloak will go on the K3s
                cluster as a Helm release in its own namespace, with values pinned
                in the repository rather than typed at a prompt.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Persistence:</span> CloudNativePG behind it
                with a PersistentVolumeClaim, because Keycloak's default in-memory
                database loses realm configuration on restart &mdash; realms,
                clients and users have to survive a pod being rescheduled.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Exposure:</span> published through
                ingress-nginx with a cert-manager certificate. Keycloak refuses to
                issue tokens over plain HTTP for good reason, so TLS is a
                prerequisite rather than a finishing touch.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">The Authorization Code Flow, End to End</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              SpendSmart &mdash; my existing expense-tracking app &mdash; becomes
              the protected resource, and stops knowing what a password is. It is
              a server-side Streamlit app, so it will be registered as a
              <span class="case-k">confidential client</span>: it can hold a
              client secret, and PKCE goes on top of that anyway, because OAuth
              2.1 requires it of every client rather than only the public ones.
            </p>
            <p class="case-text">
              The split matters. The authorization code travels back through the
              browser because that is the only channel the user's session can
              cross &mdash; but a code is single-use and worthless without the
              verifier and the secret. The exchange for actual tokens happens on a
              direct back-channel call from the server to Keycloak, so the access
              token never touches the browser at all.
            </p>
            <ol class="case-steps">
              <li>
                Unauthenticated request hits the app; it redirects the browser to
                the Keycloak realm's authorization endpoint with a client ID,
                requested scopes, a redirect URI and a PKCE challenge.
              </li>
              <li>
                Keycloak authenticates the user itself and redirects back with a
                single-use authorization code.
              </li>
              <li>
                The app exchanges that code, plus the PKCE verifier, for an ID
                token and an access token at the token endpoint &mdash; a
                back-channel call the browser never sees.
              </li>
              <li>
                The app validates the token signature against the realm's JWKS
                endpoint, checks issuer, audience and expiry, and maps the realm
                roles in the token onto its own authorisation rules.
              </li>
            </ol>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">What I Expect to Break, and Why</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              These are the four failures this setup is known for. Writing them
              down before I hit them is the point &mdash; the log of what actually
              happened goes here on Sunday.
            </p>
            <ul class="case-list">
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Redirect URI mismatch:</span> the most common
                failure in the entire protocol, and deliberately unforgiving
                &mdash; the registered URI has to match exactly, because a loose
                match is an open redirect and a token theft primitive.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Proxy headers:</span> behind an ingress,
                Keycloak builds its issuer and redirect URLs from the forwarded
                headers. Until proxy mode is configured correctly it will generate
                internal-looking URLs that the browser cannot reach.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Clock skew:</span> token validation compares
                <code>exp</code> and <code>iat</code> against the verifying
                machine's clock. A drifting node produces tokens that are somehow
                already expired, or not yet valid.
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="case-k">Header buffer sizes:</span> tokens carrying a
                lot of role claims will outgrow the ingress's default header
                buffer, which surfaces as an opaque 502 rather than anything
                mentioning tokens.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">
              Passwordless &mdash; The Part I Actually Care About
            </h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              Once the realm is running, I am adding a WebAuthn Passwordless
              Authenticator as an alternative to the password step, and
              registering a passkey against it. The goal is a login to SpendSmart
              with no password anywhere in the flow.
            </p>
            <p class="case-text">
              The property that makes this more than a convenience feature: a
              passkey is cryptographically bound to the Relying Party ID. A
              lookalike domain cannot produce a valid assertion, because the
              credential simply will not sign for the wrong origin. That is
              phishing resistance neither SMS codes nor TOTP can offer &mdash;
              both will happily authenticate you to an attacker's proxy.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">OIDC and SAML, Side by Side</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              I am registering a second client using SAML 2.0 against the same
              realm specifically so that I end up with a first-hand opinion rather
              than a memorised one.
            </p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
              <div class="border border-gray-800 p-4">
                <div class="mono-label-red !mb-3 !text-[10px]">[ OIDC ]</div>
                <p class="case-text !text-xs !mb-0">
                  JSON and JWTs over a REST-shaped flow. Light enough for SPAs and
                  mobile clients, PKCE covers public clients that cannot keep a
                  secret. The default choice for anything new.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="border border-gray-800 p-4">
                <div class="mono-label-red !mb-3 !text-[10px]">[ SAML 2.0 ]</div>
                <p class="case-text !text-xs !mb-0">
                  Signed XML assertions over browser POST bindings. Heavier, but
                  it is what a large amount of existing enterprise software
                  actually speaks &mdash; which is why "legacy" is a description,
                  not a reason to avoid learning it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <h3 class="case-h3">What I Expect This to Change</h3>
            <p class="case-text">
              The interesting part is not the login screen. It is treating
              identity as infrastructure &mdash; something you deploy, secure,
              persist, expose over TLS and monitor like anything else &mdash; so
              that every other service stops inventing its own answer to the same
              question.
            </p>
          </section>

          <div class="pt-2">
            <p
              class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 leading-relaxed"
            >
              Repository and measured results land here Sun 9 Aug.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

